question:
Now operationalize the report by creating a routine that scans up to 15 most recent orders (highest order id first) and flags suspicious orders where DELIVERY is earlier than ORDERED (lexicographically, since both are stored as strings). For each such order, print a warning line with the order id, customer name, ordered, and delivery. Also print a final count of suspicious orders. Make sure the routine can optionally focus on a single customer id.

Requirements:
Procedure Name: flag_suspicious_deliveries
Parameters:
p_customer_id IN ORACLE_SQL.CUSTOMERS.id%TYPE
Output:
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Suspicious delivery scan (max rows = ' || co_report_limit || ')');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Focus customer_id = ' || TO_CHAR(p_customer_id));
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('WARNING: ORDER_ID=' || r.order_id || ' | CUSTOMER=' || r.customer_name || ' | ORDERED=' || r.ordered_str || ' | DELIVERY=' || r.delivery_str);
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Suspicious orders found: ' || TO_CHAR(lv_suspicious_cnt));
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No orders available to scan.');
Exception Handling:
WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN: 'No data found.'
WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN: 'Multiple rows returned.'
WHEN OTHERS THEN: 'Unexpected error.'

reference_answer:
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE flag_suspicious_deliveries(
  p_customer_id IN ORACLE_SQL.CUSTOMERS.id%TYPE
) IS
  co_report_limit CONSTANT PLS_INTEGER := 15; -- Limit output for operational scan

  lv_suspicious_cnt PLS_INTEGER := 0;
  lv_rows_scanned   PLS_INTEGER := 0;
BEGIN
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Suspicious delivery scan (max rows = ' || co_report_limit || ')');
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Focus customer_id = ' || TO_CHAR(p_customer_id));

  FOR r IN (
    SELECT *
    FROM (
      SELECT o.id AS order_id,
             c.name AS customer_name,
             o.ordered AS ordered_str,
             o.delivery AS delivery_str
        FROM ORACLE_SQL.ORDERS o
        INNER JOIN ORACLE_SQL.CUSTOMERS c
          ON c.id = o.customer_id
       WHERE (p_customer_id IS NULL OR o.customer_id = p_customer_id)
       ORDER BY o.id DESC
    )
    WHERE ROWNUM <= co_report_limit
  ) LOOP
    lv_rows_scanned := lv_rows_scanned + 1;

    -- Since dates are strings in this dataset, use lexicographic check as a pragmatic quality rule.
    IF r.delivery_str < r.ordered_str THEN
      lv_suspicious_cnt := lv_suspicious_cnt + 1;
      DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(
        'WARNING: ORDER_ID=' || r.order_id ||
        ' | CUSTOMER=' || r.customer_name ||
        ' | ORDERED=' || r.ordered_str ||
        ' | DELIVERY=' || r.delivery_str
      );
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  IF lv_rows_scanned = 0 THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No orders available to scan.');
  END IF;

  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Suspicious orders found: ' || TO_CHAR(lv_suspicious_cnt));

EXCEPTION
  WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No data found.');
  WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Multiple rows returned.');
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Unexpected error.');
END flag_suspicious_deliveries;
/

